# Function Parameter & Return Annotations ✍️

Every parameter in TypeScript can be given an explicit type annotation, followed by a return type annotation:

```typescript
// (parameter: type): returnType
function add(x: number, y: number): number {
  return x + y;
}

const greeting = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`;

console.log("Sum:", add(10, 25));
console.log(greeting("Marcus"));
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
function add(x: number, y: number): number {
  return x + y;
}

const greeting = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`;

console.log("Sum:", add(10, 25));
console.log("Greeting:", greeting("Marcus"));
FULL_CODE_END -->

If a function body attempts to return the wrong type, TypeScript immediately flags a compile-time error!

# Optional Parameters (`param?: type`) ❓

Use a question mark `?` to mark a parameter as optional. Optional parameters must always appear **after** all required parameters:

```typescript
function buildAddress(street: string, city: string, aptNumber?: string): string {
  if (aptNumber) {
    return `${street}, Apt ${aptNumber}, ${city}`;
  }
  return `${street}, ${city}`;
}

console.log(buildAddress("123 Main St", "Springfield"));
console.log(buildAddress("456 Elm St", "Gotham", "4B"));
```

