# The `void` Return Type 🚫

`void` indicates that a function does **not return a value** (or returns `undefined`):

```typescript
function logSystemStatus(status: string): void {
  console.log(`[STATUS]: ${status}`);
  // No return statement!
}

logSystemStatus("Database connected");
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
function logSystemStatus(status: string): void {
  console.log(`[STATUS LOG]: ${status}`);
}

logSystemStatus("Service online and healthy");
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 💡 **`void` vs `undefined`:** In TypeScript, a function with return type `void` does not have to explicitly write `return undefined;`. It simply finishes without returning anything.

# The `never` Return Type ⛔

The `never` type represents a function that **never returns anything at all**!

This happens in two scenarios:
1. The function throws an error/exception every single time.
2. The function contains an infinite execution loop.

```typescript
function failProcess(errorMessage: string): never {
  throw new Error(`Fatal system failure: ${errorMessage}`);
}

function runInfiniteLoop(): never {
  while (true) {
    // Never exits!
  }
}
```

